<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dalian</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dairen, Dalny</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dalian is a port city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoning">Liaoning</a> province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, near the southern tip of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> peninsula. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> founded it in 1898 as the commercial port of Dalny within its Kwantung lease, and after 1905 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> developed it as Dairen, headquarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-manchuria-railway">South Manchuria Railway Company</a> and one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>'s busiest harbours. It returned to China after 1945 and now includes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lushunkou">Lüshunkou</a>, the former Port Arthur.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dalian.jpg" alt="Dalian" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fumihiko Ueno · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xigang,_Dalian,_Liaoning,_China_-_panoramio_(18).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dalian is a prefecture-level, sub-provincial port city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoning">Liaoning</a> province, on the southern tip of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> peninsula. It is the southernmost city in Liaoning and in Northeast China as a whole, and shares maritime boundaries with cities across Liaodong Bay and the Bohai Strait and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a> across the Korea Bay. Its climate is humid continental, with warm, wet summers under the East Asian monsoon and cold, dry winters. The municipal area consists of Dalian proper and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lushunkou">Lüshunkou</a>, formerly the city of Lüshun, known in Western and Russian sources as Port Arthur, about 40 kilometres away.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> and Han empires annexed the Dalian peninsula from a Korean state, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-kingdoms">Sixteen Kingdoms</a> era the Korean state of Goguryeo controlled the region. In the 1880s the Qing government built bridges and heavily fortified the peninsula. Mining camps on the northern coast of Dalian Bay grew into the small town of Qingniwa, from which modern Dalian originated. The British briefly occupied Qingniwa in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-opium-war">Second Opium War</a> in 1858 and returned it in 1860. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> overcame the Chinese defences in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shimonoseki">Treaty of Shimonoseki</a> of 17 April 1895 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> ceded the Liaodong Peninsula, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penghu">Penghu</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> coerced a lease of the area from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> in 1898 and built a commercial town called Dalny, a name meaning far-away. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a> of 1904-1905 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> occupied the area as the Kwantung Leased Territory and renamed the city Dairen, the Japanese reading of the Chinese name of Dalian Bay. Shipbuilding and locomotive works were already located in the city before and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sino-japanese-war">Second Sino-Japanese War</a>. Many farmers and fishers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> settled in the area during the Chuang Guandong migration, and the Dalian dialect, a Jiaoliao Mandarin speech, has a few loanwords from Japanese and Russian.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s surrender, the Soviet Manchurian offensive took the city on 22 August 1945. Unlike many cities of the northeast, it never reverted to Nationalist authority. The Soviets and the Chinese Communists cooperated to develop its industry and port, which had been relatively undamaged in the war, and in 1945 the first Communist mayor of the Lüda Administrative Office was appointed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Dalian is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoning">Liaoning</a>'s second largest city after the provincial capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shenyang">Shenyang</a>, with 7,450,785 inhabitants at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. It is a shipping, financial and logistics centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>. Zhongshan Square, laid out by the Russians, is surrounded by classical buildings that the Japanese built in the first half of the 20th century. Dalian University of Technology and Dalian Maritime University, founded in 1909, are among its universities.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dalian” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the Treaty of Portsmouth in 1905 Japan took over Russia's lease of Kwantung Oblast, including Port Arthur (Ryojun) and <b>Dalny</b> (Dairen), together with the railway south of Changchun.</blockquote><a href="/state/kwantung-leased-territory">Kwantung Leased Territory</a></li><li><blockquote>Russia fortified Port Arthur as an ice-free Pacific naval base and built a commercial port at <b>Dalny</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kwantung-oblast">Kwantung Oblast</a></li></ul></section>
